WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:05.552
<v Examiner>Define a licit drug and an illicit drug, with an example of each.

2
00:00:05.552 --> 00:00:06.189
<v Examiner>Two marks.

3
00:00:06.639 --> 00:00:08.464
The hook: licit is lawful.

4
00:00:08.464 --> 00:00:09.798
Illicit is illegal.

5
00:00:09.798 --> 00:00:12.185
And every Class A drug is illicit.

6
00:00:12.635 --> 00:00:17.124
A licit drug is a medication or drug that is lawful and permitted.

7
00:00:17.124 --> 00:00:22.770
That covers every schedule of medicine you met in Module 1: prescription medicines,

8
00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:27.735
restricted medicines, pharmacy-only medicines and general sale medicines.

9
00:00:27.735 --> 00:00:32.292
And it covers some controlled drugs, some from Class B and Class C,

10
00:00:32.292 --> 00:00:34.265
because those are prescribed.

11
00:00:34.265 --> 00:00:36.509
Licit drugs can still be misused.

12
00:00:36.509 --> 00:00:38.210
They just aren't illegal.

13
00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:41.747
Her examples: tobacco and nicotine, including vapes.

14
00:00:41.747 --> 00:00:42.291
Alcohol.

15
00:00:42.291 --> 00:00:45.284
Anti-anxiety medicines like benzodiazepines.

16
00:00:45.284 --> 00:00:47.188
Analgesics like the opioids.

17
00:00:47.188 --> 00:00:50.317
Cough and cold medicines like pseudoephedrine.

18
00:00:50.317 --> 00:00:52.153
And inhalants and solvents.

19
00:00:52.603 --> 00:00:56.259
An illicit drug is unlawful, or otherwise not permitted.

20
00:00:56.259 --> 00:01:00.828
That's every Class A controlled drug, some Class B, and a few Class C.

21
00:01:00.828 --> 00:01:04.614
Methamphetamine is the example she uses, and it's Class A.

22
00:01:06.064 --> 00:01:08.965
Remember the classes from the legal lecture.

23
00:01:08.965 --> 00:01:13.843
The Misuse of Drugs Act 1975 sorts controlled drugs into Class A, B and C.

24
00:01:13.843 --> 00:01:18.194
The higher the class, the higher the risk of misuse and addiction.

25
00:01:18.194 --> 00:01:20.962
Class A, very high risk, never prescribed.

26
00:01:20.962 --> 00:01:25.775
Class B, like morphine, prescribed, but locked in the double-locked safe.

27
00:01:25.775 --> 00:01:28.016
Class C, lower risk, like codeine.

28
00:01:28.466 --> 00:01:30.303
Where the marks go missing.

29
00:01:30.303 --> 00:01:32.887
One: controlled does not mean illicit.

30
00:01:32.887 --> 00:01:34.792
Her own formative asks this.

31
00:01:34.792 --> 00:01:40.777
Controlled drugs are substances under special legal controls to reduce misuse and abuse.

32
00:01:40.777 --> 00:01:44.858
Morphine is controlled, and it's licit when it's prescribed.

33
00:01:44.858 --> 00:01:46.763
Two: one more word she uses.

34
00:01:46.763 --> 00:01:50.912
All medications are drugs, but not all drugs are medications.

35
00:01:50.912 --> 00:01:55.333
In Aotearoa, when the use is a worry, people often say substance.

36
00:01:55.783 --> 00:01:56.362
<v Examiner>Once more.

37
00:01:56.362 --> 00:01:57.405
<v Examiner>Licit and illicit.

38
00:01:59.855 --> 00:02:03.779
Licit means lawful and permitted: prescription, restricted,

39
00:02:03.779 --> 00:02:09.565
pharmacy-only and general sale medicines, and some controlled drugs from Class B and C.

40
00:02:09.565 --> 00:02:12.359
Alcohol, or an opioid taken as prescribed.

41
00:02:12.359 --> 00:02:18.278
Illicit means unlawful or not permitted: every Class A drug, some Class B, a few Class C.

42
00:02:18.278 --> 00:02:19.941
Methamphetamine, Class A.

43
00:02:19.941 --> 00:02:22.468
Controlled is not the same as illicit.
